# Line-ending policy for a repository whose contents are SIGNED.
#
# Every `.txt` here has a detached Ed25519 signature in its `.txt.sig`
# sibling, taken over the file's raw bytes (v1 signs
# `filename + "\n" + content`). End-of-line translation changes those
# bytes, so ANY translation invalidates every signature in the repo.
#
# Without this file that is not hypothetical: git's Windows default is
# `core.autocrlf=true`, so a plain
#
#     git clone https://github.com/pparesh25/OpenTraderPro-MarketPlace
#
# on Windows rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout and all 33 files then fail
# verification — the app reports them as TAMPERED, which reads as an
# attack rather than as a checkout setting. Measured on a Windows box
# 2026-08-05: 0 of 33 verified from a default clone, 33 of 33 from
# `git -c core.autocrlf=false clone`. CI never caught it because the
# workflow runs on `ubuntu-latest`, where the default does nothing.

# Default for the repository: normalise text in the object store and
# check it out with LF everywhere. Nothing matched by this line is
# signed, so this is ordinary cross-platform hygiene.
*               text=auto eol=lf

# Signed material — exempt from the line above, and from translation in
# BOTH directions.
#
# `-text` is deliberate rather than `eol=lf`. `eol=lf` would fix the
# checkout side while leaving git's checkin-side normalisation in the
# path, which is the same defect pointing the other way: a maintainer
# who signs a working-tree file that happens to carry CRLF would have
# git silently store LF, and the signature they just published would be
# invalid for everyone — including their own next clone. `-text` means
# the bytes in the working tree are exactly the bytes in the object
# store, which is the property a signature over raw bytes needs.
#
# `-eol` is not redundant next to `-text`. The `*` line above sets
# `eol=lf`, and git documents `eol` as "effectively setting the text
# attribute" — so leaving it inherited makes byte-fidelity here depend
# on git's internal precedence between two attributes that are saying
# opposite things. Measured on git 2.x, `-text` does win (a CRLF `.txt`
# stages with its CRLF intact either way), but that is an
# implementation detail to observe, not one to build signature
# integrity on. Unsetting `eol` says what this line means, and makes
# `git check-attr eol -- <file>` report it honestly instead of
# answering "lf" for a file nothing is allowed to translate.
#
# `diff` is set back on because `-text` otherwise makes git treat these
# as binary and stop showing diffs. The marketplace's first promise is
# that every shipped file is readable, reviewable source (README
# §"Source-readable is the primary defence"), and that has to hold in
# `git diff` and in PR review, not just in an editor.
*.txt           -text -eol diff
*.sig           -text -eol diff

# Tracked compiled artefacts (`tests/__pycache__/*.pyc`, committed
# before .gitignore covered them). `text=auto` above would almost
# certainly detect these as binary on its own; saying so explicitly
# means correctness here does not rest on a heuristic.
*.pyc           binary
